WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.778
AI models are shifting from chatting to acting.

2
00:00:03.828 --> 00:00:09.361
Agents now plan multi-step research tasks, call tools, and run models on their own.

3
00:00:09.761 --> 00:00:11.795
Agriculture could gain the most.

4
00:00:11.845 --> 00:00:19.124
But its knowledge is scattered across journals, extension bulletins, technical reports, field videos and databases.

5
00:00:19.174 --> 00:00:24.431
And the data, code and models behind many papers are simply out of an agent's reach.

6
00:00:24.831 --> 00:00:26.971
This perspective proposes a fix:

7
00:00:27.021 --> 00:00:34.871
FAIR principles for agriculture AI agents, extending the original FAIR data principles, and FAIR for research software.

8
00:00:36.521 --> 00:00:38.727
The new bar is agent-actionability.

9
00:00:38.777 --> 00:00:47.309
Can an agent discover a knowledge unit, access it, execute it, trace its provenance, and interpret the output, with no human in the loop?

10
00:00:49.259 --> 00:00:52.451
The four letters stay, but each one is upgraded.

11
00:00:52.501 --> 00:00:59.001
Findable now means version-level identifiers, ontology links, and registries that agents can query.

12
00:00:59.401 --> 00:01:07.026
Accessible means open protocols, logins that agents can pass, and interfaces that run a model, not just download it.

13
00:01:07.076 --> 00:01:11.553
Interoperable adds shared schemas and version-controlled provenance.

14
00:01:11.603 --> 00:01:15.358
Reusable adds machine-readable licences and runtime logs.

15
00:01:15.408 --> 00:01:19.425
Sixteen principles in all, each scored unmet, partial, or met.

16
00:01:21.075 --> 00:01:22.520
In practice, for text:

17
00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:33.825
publish in structured formats such as LaTeX, tag concepts with ontologies like Agro-voc, state the licence for every version, and link each figure to the script that made it.

18
00:01:34.225 --> 00:01:35.487
For audio and video:

19
00:01:35.537 --> 00:01:44.543
record the speaker on a separate track, keep the camera steady, align slides with the transcript by time, and have a human verify the transcript.

20
00:01:46.193 --> 00:01:55.252
For databases and models, the path runs from web pages built for people, to standard APIs, to Model Context Protocol servers.

21
00:01:55.302 --> 00:01:57.854
Code gets packaged as agent skills:

22
00:01:57.904 --> 00:02:01.397
tools plus instructions, with a log of every run.

23
00:02:01.797 --> 00:02:03.817
Early evidence is encouraging.

24
00:02:03.867 --> 00:02:13.501
A poultry nutrition agent with curated tools reached about ninety-six percent numeric accuracy, against thirty-nine percent for an unaided model.

25
00:02:15.601 --> 00:02:16.867
There are tensions.

26
00:02:16.917 --> 00:02:19.236
Small groups may lack the capacity.

27
00:02:19.286 --> 00:02:22.197
Workflows come to depend on a few providers.

28
00:02:22.247 --> 00:02:25.421
Agents can amplify errors as easily as insights.

29
00:02:25.471 --> 00:02:27.922
And credit for curators is unsettled.

30
00:02:27.972 --> 00:02:31.935
The authors call the framework necessary, but not sufficient.

31
00:02:34.335 --> 00:02:35.160
The message:

32
00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:40.141
make agricultural research usable by agents, not just readable by people.

33
00:02:40.191 --> 00:02:43.843
Otherwise, today's results become tomorrow's dark data.
